  "notes": "The lane's lo-fi auteur. `confidence` is **low** because the research rates the entry medium-low — the method and the collaborators are sourced, but \"tempo/harmonic values are inference and marked\". The entry's own headline claim is unusual and is what this model encodes: \"the defining parameter is fidelity, not pattern — a distorted, clipped, room-mic'd or drum-machine kit recorded to a home 4-track, snare on 2&4 but saturated to the point of losing transient, hats crushed or absent\". Fidelity is not a drum field, so it is expressed as its consequences: the narrowest, loudest velocity tiers in the section (a clipped kit has almost no dynamic range), the lowest `velocityVar` here, a hat that is neither continuous nor busy at a fill density of 0.08, and a near-empty percussion layer. Harmony is the two garage-soul families the entry writes out — I–IV and I–♭III–IV — with the highest triad weight in the section, because the research says \"two- and three-chord garage-soul progressions… with no modulation\". Melody is \"mid-tenor into falsetto, short-phrase, hooky, with hummed and wordless passages\", so it is a riff loop over two-and-four-bar phrases. Bass is \"overdriven, mixed hot and slightly out of tune with itself, root-and-fifth\". The arrangement encodes \"very short tracks (many under two minutes), abrupt starts and stops, no bridges\" — no bridge section exists in either structure. ⚠ Every drum, melody and bass number here is INFERRED. ⚠ The Roots' clean band re-recording of the same material is documented but not encoded; this model states the 4-track version.",
